# Notes/Sync stack secrets (services VM) — rmfakecloud + Syncthing
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/notes-sync.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys —
# the stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting. Verified empirically (finding #30): Docker
# Compose interpolates env_file values, so an unquoted '$' is expanded away and
# the container receives a DIFFERENT string. None of the generate commands
# below emit '$'.
#
# ⚠ ONE OF THESE MUST NEVER BE ROTATED, and its unset case is worse than its
# rotated case. See JWT_SECRET_KEY.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# rmfakecloud
#
# 🚨 JWT_SECRET_KEY — NEVER ROTATE, AND NEVER LEAVE UNSET.
#
# It signs both the tablet device tokens and the web session cookie, stretched
# through PBKDF2-SHA256. Rotating it invalidates EVERY TABLET ENROLMENT — each
# device has to be re-paired by hand.
#
# The unset case is the dangerous one because it is not an error. config.go
# generates 32 random bytes, logs
#     WARN The authentication will fail the next time you start the server!
#     WARN JWT_SECRET_KEY was not set! The following was autogenerated
# and carries on. The container is healthy, the tablet syncs happily, and the
# NEXT RESTART silently invalidates every token. It presents as "the tablet
# stopped syncing after a reboot", with nothing in any log at that moment to
# explain it.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JWT_SECRET_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rmfakecloud admin.
#
# 🚨 THERE IS NO REGISTRATION STEP AND NO ADMIN-CREATION API. On an empty data
# directory the FIRST POST to /ui/api/login creates an admin out of whatever
# credentials arrive, and there is no switch that closes that window. Being
# first is the security model — notes-sync-init races to be first, and the
# loopback-only publish is what stops anything else winning. Corollary: never
# point Caddy at this container before the admin exists.
#
# ⚠ NO PLUS-ADDRESSING. rmfakecloud's sanitizeEmail runs on CREATE but not on
# LOGIN, and its whitelist regexp `[^a-zA-Z0-9.@-_]+` parses `@-_` as an ASCII
# range that does not include '+'. So idan+rm@example.com is stored as
# idanrm@example.com and every later login fails with a bare 401.
# notes-sync-init refuses a '+' up front rather than creating an account that
# can never be logged into.
# Generate the password: openssl rand -base64 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RMFAKECLOUD_ADMIN_EMAIL=idan@idanreed.com
RMFAKECLOUD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Syncthing
#
# ⚠ STGUIAPIKEY is a FULL-ADMIN credential that bypasses GUI authentication
# entirely — it is not a second-class token. It must live here and never in the
# repo, and the Caddy vhost must never forward a client-supplied X-API-Key.
#
# ⚠ Rotating it REVOKES NOTHING on its own. guiconfiguration.go accepts either
# this value OR the random key `syncthing generate` wrote into config.xml
# (`case c.APIKey, os.Getenv("STGUIAPIKEY")`), so both stay valid in parallel.
# A real revocation also needs PATCH /rest/config/gui to replace the stored
# key.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# ST_GUI_USER / ST_GUI_PASSWORD are seeded by the syncthing_generate one-shot.
# Note IsAuthEnabled() is `AuthMode == ldap || (User != "" && Password != "")`
# — HALF A CREDENTIAL IS NO CREDENTIAL, so leaving either blank turns GUI auth
# off entirely rather than failing. The GUI also sits behind Caddy forward
# auth; this is the second lock, for anything reaching the port directly.
# Generate the password: openssl rand -base64 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
STGUIAPIKEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32
ST_GUI_USER=idan
ST_GUI_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT SECRETS, but worth knowing while you are in here:
#
#   - key.pem in /mnt/fast/syncthing/config IS the device identity. It is not
#     an env var and cannot be one. Losing or regenerating it changes the
#     device ID and every peer must re-accept the "new" device — treat it like
#     headscale's noise_private.key. `syncthing generate` refuses to overwrite
#     an existing key, which is what protects it across redeploys.
#   - Peer devices are added by device ID from BOTH sides. That mutual
#     certificate authentication is exactly what makes publishing 22000 on
#     0.0.0.0 safe, so do not pre-seed devices to save a step.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
